          "answer": "Knee pain can be urgent, so please see a clinician promptly if your knee becomes hot, red, swollen and very painful, especially with fever. The osteoarthritis guidance says this \"suggests infection or crystal arthritis and needs urgent assessment.\" Also get prompt review for sudden locking or giving way of the joint, prolonged morning stiffness, pain in several small joints on both sides, or whole-body symptoms, which can suggest inflammatory arthritis. Weakness, numbness or new nerve symptoms with back or spine pain also need review. The sources I could access did not cover sudden swelling, being unable to bear weight, or pain after an injury, so I cannot cite guidance for those. They are still reasonable reasons to see a clinician soon rather than wait. The emergency guidance I found covers serious infections such as sepsis and meningitis, where fever with joint or muscle pain can be a warning sign. Sepsis signs include confusion, very fast breathing, and feeling more unwell than ever before. Meningitis signs include fever with a severe headache and stiff neck.\n\nFor core treatment, the osteoarthritis guidance describes education and pacing, suitable footwear, walking aids where useful, heat or cold for symptom relief, and weight management where relevant. Medicines are described as supporting measures rather than the foundation, with topical products preferred for accessible joints. Joint replacement is considered when pain and limitation persist despite good non-surgical care.\n\nThe sources disagree on emphasis. One reading puts these self-management measures and weight management at the centre. A separate guideline (Rheumatology and orthopedic society guidance, \"Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management\") describes exercise as the base of treatment, with creams, tablets, steroid injections and duloxetine added to an exercise program. It says opioids are not recommended for osteoarthritis and that most bodies do not recommend glucosamine or chondroitin. I could not retrieve its details on exercise, weight loss or education, and I cannot rate how strong the evidence is for any of these options. Your clinician can explain which approach fits you and why.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
